The actual fun-de-mental stack

I think it’s this:

\boxed{
\text{Notice}
\rightarrow
\text{Compare}
\rightarrow
\text{Change}
\rightarrow
\text{Repeat}
\rightarrow
\text{Expect}
\rightarrow
\text{Break}
\rightarrow
\text{Play}
}

Or even smaller:

Same. Different. Again.

Those three generate an absurd amount of mathematics.

Same gives equivalence and invariants.

Different gives distance, delta, contrast, ordering.

Again gives recurrence, rhythm, sequence, iteration.

And then:

\text{Same + Different}
\rightarrow \text{analogy}

\text{Same + Again}
\rightarrow \text{rhyme}

\text{Different + Again}
\rightarrow \text{change}

\text{Same + Different + Again}
\rightarrow \boxed{\text{pattern}}

That’s the bedrock.

And there’s your fun-de-mental pun too: before mathematics is formal, it’s the mind having fun noticing what remains the same while everything else moves.